WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.835
[whoosh]

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:06.206
- Elvin is part of a series of
events puzzling investigators--

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.341
nine shark attacks
along the Southeast

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:14.447
Florida coast in 2017, more
than double the average.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:17.650
And with leads coming up
short, some local authorities

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:20.420
come up with their own
theories for the spike.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:24.324
Vero Beach Ocean Rescue's
Eric Toomsoo explains.

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:27.460
- In my experience, it's based
on a few different issues--

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.562
people in the water, number one.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.031
Number two, how
clear the water is.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:33.867
Usually there's--
it's murky water

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:34.968
when we've had an incident.

00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:37.470
[splashing through water]

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:41.841
- Murky water-- it seems
like an innocuous detail.

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:46.413
But witness Jessica Veach echoes
the idea that it's important.

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:50.150
- There's a lot of
sand being churned,

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:52.519
which makes it hard
for the shark to see

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.421
what it's actually fighting.

00:00:56.689 --> 00:00:59.492
- Could the shark's ability
to see in murky water

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.431
be the one thing that ties
all the attacks together?

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:08.201
According to Dr. Huber,
sharks are adept at navigating

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.369
murky water--

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.605
relying on other senses.

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:15.208
- In murky water sharks,
can lean more heavily

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:17.944
upon the lateral line and
the electrosensory systems

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:19.546
in order to locate their prey.

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.582
- But he does think there is
a unique limit to a shark's

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:27.153
vision that could have played
a role in some of the attacks.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.956
And it has to do
with available light.

00:01:30.023 --> 00:01:32.525
- We're bringing
out a spiny dogfish.

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:35.595
- To investigate, Doctor
Huber performs an eye

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:38.898
dissection using this
common shark found

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.501
in waters all over the world.

00:01:41.568 --> 00:01:43.436
- I'm getting towards
the back of the eyeball.

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:49.476
We'll be able to free it up
and remove it from the skull.

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.278
This is the back
half of the eyeball.

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.481
One of the really interesting
things about sharks' eyes

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:56.683
is that they have a layer
of reflective crystals

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.918
that coat the back of
the eye with a thing

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:00.386
called a tapetum lucidum.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.689
And what this does is it
actually causes night shine.

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.091
You may have seen night
shine on your cat or your dog

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.526
or on a variety of
different animals.

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:10.463
- In humans, light passes
through the eye once.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.667
But for sharks in dark
waters, the tapetum lucidum

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.269
bounces the light back--

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:20.306
letting the eye process
images a second time.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.208
- In layman's terms,
this basically means

00:02:22.275 --> 00:02:25.345
that sharks have night vision.

00:02:25.411 --> 00:02:28.948
- But as the sun begins to
rise, a shark's eye must

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:32.986
react to the increasing light.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.287
- You can think
of when you first

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:36.022
open your eyes in the
morning and things

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:37.557
seem a little bit too bright.

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:39.692
After a few seconds, your
eyes accommodate to the amount

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.461
of light in the room
and then you can see

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:44.531
things as you normally would.

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:47.667
And in sharks, this process
can take anywhere from a half

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:50.370
an hour to potentially
a couple of hours.

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:54.040
- It's a process called
visual accommodation.

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:58.378
During these periods of changing
light, like dusk or dawn,

00:02:58.444 --> 00:03:01.481
a shark's vision
may be compromised--

00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:04.150
potentially leaving
swimmers vulnerable.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:09.489
During these times, sharks
are very, very active

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:12.625
looking for their prey, which
would increase the probability

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:14.727
of a shark attack if
people were in the water

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:15.728
at those same times.

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:22.702
[music playing]

